Regarding the memory overhead this adds for every Control, I wonder whether dynamically allocating the data only for Controls actually using the rendering transform would be a good solution. Basically moving the data into a struct and storing only a pointer in the Control:

```cpp
class Control : public CanvasItem {
	...

	struct RenderTransform {
		Vector2 offset;
		Vector2 scale = Vector2(1, 1);
		real_t rotation = 0.0;
		...
	};

	RenderTransform *render_transform = nullptr;
	...
```
Such data could be allocated/deallocated on enabling/disabling render transform for the given Control. This would result in non-locallity of data though, hence not sure if this is a good approach. Just throwing it up for consideration.
(I'm guessing relatively small percent of Controls would use render transforms, so saving the memory probably makes sense.)

@Iniquitatis It's pretty standard for editors to show something that will be placed when you use LMB (i.e. tilemap), but I believe it's better to judge on a case by case basis. So if you think it's bothersome, please do tell me.

> because mouse pointer itself should point to an exact point on the screen

It does, in the above example the cross is a bit away because snapping is enabled.

```cpp
Vector2i grid_steps = Vector2i(0, 0);
if (snap_enabled || Input::get_singleton()->is_key_pressed(Key::CTRL)) {
	// If snapping, find an appropriate grid size.
	if (snap_count == 2) {
		grid_steps = Vector2i(2, 2);
	} else if (snap_count == 3 || snap_count == 6 || snap_count == 9) {
		grid_steps = Vector2i(3, 3);
	} else if (snap_count % 5 == 0) {
		grid_steps = Vector2i(5, 5);
	} else if (snap_count % 4 == 0) {
		grid_steps = Vector2i(4, 4);
	}
}

if (grid_steps == Vector2i(0, 0)) {
	// Use a default/fallback value.
	grid_steps = Vector2i(4, 2);
}
```

I tried the "adjust grid based on snap divisions" suggestion with this code and it looked worse with it than without it. Not implementing it.

@azagaya note that i never said "I don't think we should have this feature"

What I want to do is to avoid putting a lot of cognitive load on users by giving them too many knobs to understand that they then don't use 80% of the time.

I really love this turbulence work and i want to see it merged too ^^

